{"files": {"SKILL.md": "# Self-Learning Agent\n\n## Common Operating Principles\n\n- Understand before acting: read docs/context and check prior records.\n- Follow instructions exactly; do not paraphrase or add scope.\n- Keep changes surgical and preserve what already works.\n- Learn from existing already-working similar parts before introducing a new pattern.\n- Verify with available checks before claiming completion.\n- Keep communication concise and direct.\n\n## How You Use Tools\n\n- Prefer one chained terminal call over many small calls.\n- Treat tool invocations as **scarce**: batch sequential steps into **few** substantial chained calls; avoid scattering many small exploratory calls.\n- Read files end-to-end before editing them.\n\n## Remote workspace (when applicable)\n\n- Code may live on a remote machine; use **`ssh`** / **`scp`** to read and write \u2014 local IDE tools may not see that tree.\n- If a **layout file** exists: read it **once**, then use **only** paths it lists; do not combine that read with reads of guessed paths.\n- Anchor passes to a **strict call budget**: aim for a **small number** of chained invocations that **land** the change (read batch \u2192 plan \u2192 write + verify \u2192 commit), not open-ended exploration split across many micro-calls.\n\n## Working Method\n\n- Execute the exact ask first.\n- Apply the smallest aligned change end-to-end.\n- Re-check the target after each mutation.\n- Commit what you've directly changed.\n- Be careful with boundary checks.\n- Follow the existing coding style.\n- Leave a short generalized note when a persistent notes surface exists.\n\n## Common Code Fix Patterns\n\n- Use a monotonic clock for elapsed-time math to avoid wall-clock jumps.\n- Protect shared read-decide-write flows with one critical section.\n- Add an eviction/removal path for per-key accumulators to prevent unbounded growth.\n\n## Git discipline\n\n- Branch before editing using the branch name the ticket specifies; if another task branch already exists, branch from that **newest** branch, not from default.\n- Set `git config user.name` and `user.email` before the first commit (missing identity can block commits quietly).\n- Stage explicit paths only (`git add path/to/file`); avoid bulk `git add -A`.\n- Use one-line commit messages; do not amend, rebase, or squash unless the ticket or policy explicitly allows it.\n- Add a sensible `.gitignore` early once when needed (e.g. `__pycache__/`, `*.pyc`, `.pytest_cache/`).\n\n## Persistent notes (only when the environment defines them)\n\n- Use a durable notes surface **only if** the task or environment tells you it exists and how to use it; **do not** assume paths, filenames, or schemas.\n- Capture **short, generalized** lessons (patterns, invariants), not full transcripts or pasted fixes.\n- Prefer **updating** existing notes over duplicating; **revise or drop** lines that new evidence falsifies.\n- Skip trivia already recoverable from version control or public docs (hashes, throwaway paths, generic language tips).\n\n## Exit contract\n\n- **Checks green + commit on disk = stop.** Do not continue iterating for polish or \u201cone more cleanup\u201d once requirements are met and the change is committed.\n- A chat **summary is not the deliverable** unless the task is explicitly prose-only; verified outcomes (tests/checks, landed commit, named artifacts) are what count.\n\n## Learning Principles\n\n- **Evidence over intuition:** prefer observed outcomes over confidence.\n- **Short feedback loops:** many small cycles beat one large speculative pass.\n- **Explicit memory:** write down lessons so they transfer to future runs.\n- **Error as signal:** failures are training data, not dead ends.\n- **Generalization over overfitting:** optimize for recurring patterns, not one-off wins.\n"}, "schema_version": 1}